SM(175,{"n":"1 Peter 1.13-25","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Peter 1.13-25.mp3","d":2534.4,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[9.04,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.56,"Okay, we'll go ahead and get started."],[22.16,"When we get started, we'll be picking up in First Epistle of Saint Peter, chapter 1, verse 13."],[31.68,"So um there's not a lot of recap to do because most of last time we were doing introduction to 1 Peter, 2 Peter, and the Epistle of St."],[43.36,"Jude, and we talked about why we were doing those, even though St."],[47.28,"Jude's epistle we took kind of out of order, and why we did that."],[51.52,"Um, not going to rehearse all that again."],[53.68,"And then we read uh those first twelve verses, which were basically the introduction to the epistle, where we talked about how in format, 1 Peter is very similar in sort of the overall layout and the epistle style to St."],[70.92,"Paul's epistles that we read over the period of a couple of years."],[76.12,"So and that that meant that in the introduction, he sort of laid out some of the themes that he's going to be returning to sort of over and over again as we work our way through the epistle."],[91.48,"And so um, what he mainly talked about in the introduction that we read last time was he was talking to, as we said, um, based on the. content of the epistle and the time in St"],[108.32,"Peter's life where this would fit to be written."],[111.62,"And the places that we saw last time it was addressed to, that these were Roman colonies started by Claudius, the same Claudius who we know expelled the Jews from Rome."],[126.58,"And Saint Peter identifies himself as writing from Rome, and so you put those things together, and it makes sense to believe that he would have written this to some of those sort of exiles."],[141.9,"And he's been using kind of diaspora language and that kind of exile language to describe, they're talking about facing hardship."],[148.02,"ing hardship, But he's been sett out over against that and suffering using Christ as an example that beyond that suffering there is hope."],[159.66,"Beyond Christ's death, there was resurrection."],[162.14,"Beyond suffering, there's hope."],[164.54,"And so connecting that then to the second coming of Christ."],[171.18,"And so we didn't read that much last time, but that's sort of, those are sort of the themes he started working with there in those early verses."],[181.58,"And that's really where we're picking up now."],[185.22,"Uh, we stopped here last time, both for time and because there's a therefore here."],[191.16,"So, having talked about hope beyond suffering."],[196.28,"Give it the examples of the prophets, who were waiting for the coming of the first coming of Christ, Christ's suffering and his glory, death, and resurrection."],[206.88,"Their suffering in Christ's second coming."],[208.96,"Now he's going to shift with that therefore to here's what you need to do now, in light of this that we've laid out, sort of in the same way that St."],[220.72,"Paul does rhetorically in his epistles."],[224.48,"So, unless there are any questions or comments or reports from foreign markets, we'll go ahead and pick up in the first epistle of St."],[237.6,"Peter, chapter 1, verse 13"],[242.84,"Therefore, gird up the loins of your mind, be sober, and rest your hope fully upon the grace that is to be brought to you at the revelation of Jesus Christ."],[256.12,"So this is an interesting turn of phrase gird up the loins of your mind."],[260.14,"You might say, I didn't know my mind had loins."],[265.98,"But um girding up your loins is a phrase that occurs several times in the Old Testament, and uh is used metaphorically, obviously it's being used metaphorically here of your mind, but refer to a literal thing."],[281.28,"Where a person's tunic or robe that was longer would be sort of folded upward, like if you were going into battle, or if you were going to have to run from battle or from something, um, you would sort of roll that up and tuck it up."],[300.52,"Um, you could find, well, I don't think you should Google anything with the word loins in it, but uh there are sort of diagrams that show how they literally did this, how they would fold it."],[312.93,"So to give you more freedom of movement."],[315.75,"So girding up your loins is about preparing yourself."],[321.45,"Preparing yourself for some kind of battle or struggle or contest or adversity."],[325.37,"So this is what he's talking about"],[330.99,"He's saying, you know, it's like prepping in our current language."],[334.77,"But he's saying not physically, but your mind."],[339.28,"Your mind. Because the struggle and the battle he's talking about here is a spiritual one, not a physical one."],[346.98,"He's not saying get ready for our revolution to overthrow our Roman overlords."],[352.44,"He's saying get ready for what lies ahead."],[358.84,"And that you do that, by being sober, which isn't just not drinking."],[366.31,"But in general, being serious, being focused."],[369.49,"Not being distracted, not letting yourself be dulled."],[374.05,"And resting your hope fully on the grace that is to be brought to you at the revelation of Christ Jesus, or of Jesus Christ."],[381.97,"So notice here, um, we'll talk about it a little here."],[387.09,"And I've said this before, we were talking about St."],[389.33,"Paul, but we very much have it our vocabulary."],[392.77,"I've even said it a couple of times tonight."],[395.17,"We talk about the second coming of Christ."],[398.29,"And the language we use sort of conveys this idea that, like at Christ's accession, ascension, he went up into the sky, like he went away somewhere, and someday he's gonna come back."],[410.53,"he's gonna come back. From wherever he is, here"],[415.19,"But he's gone right now."],[418.2,"And obviously, that's not what we actually believe."],[421.58,"So, like every liturgy, we say Christ is in our midst."],[425.98,"We say that based on Christ's promise, wherever two or three are gathered in my name, I will be there."],[430.31,"So he's not off somewhere, in the meantime, and so even though that's how we've come to sort of translate it in that misleading way, the words that are actually used in the New Testament, as we've seen."],[444.33,"So so like we saw St."],[445.45,"Paul referring to Christ's glorious appearing, his appearing in glory."],[450.3,"Appearing. Meaning it's not that he's off somewhere, he's here, we just can't see him."],[455.58,"Tactally, physically, materially right now."],[459.03,"Um, and we have this language here, at the revelation of Jesus Christ, when Jesus Christ is revealed."],[466.03,"This is he's sort of veiled from us right now, or we don't see him, but he will be revealed."],[470.83,"And even the word, the Greek word that's most commonly translated uh as as uh referring to as second coming, the Greek word parousia, um, doesn't really mean that."],[486.43,"Um, you may, if you know a little bit about Greek, the usia part means being, and then there's para, there's a a preposition attached to the front."],[496.95,"And parousia, what it really means is sort of presence."],[502.25,"Being present. Well, how did that get translated as second coming?"],[507.63,"Well, if someone becomes present, it can be translated as like arrival."],[514.81,"So that was kind of how it ended up getting translated into English as the idea of second coming."],[520.97,"But that arrival idea, someone becoming present can also be like appearing or manifesting."],[529.23,"Like there are other ways to understand that."],[531.65,"That Christ is going to again become present in a different way than he is now."],[537.81,"And so St"],[538.19,"That's what the Greek wording there is really aimed at."],[543.55,"Peter is going to talk about this in the same way."],[545.87,"He very much does not want to say to people who are suffering, who are struggling, and who are facing difficulties, yeah, Christ is far away, but don't worry, he's coming back."],[555.4,"That's very much not what he wants to say to them."],[558.28,"Not least because it's not true, but also because that's not exactly reassuring and bolstering up your hope."],[565.29,"But he's here, but the day is coming when his presence here with us is going to be manifest to the whole world."],[574.27,"And so that's how he talks about it."],[580.19,"Well, that's what he uses here, the revelation."],[581.23,"So, yeah, glorious appearing, St"],[582.47,"Yeah. Yeah. Paul here, his revelation, other places with Parocia, his arrival, appearance, it's right, that idea."],[594.15,"This is an essential element that the unbelievers are going to see him."],[600.39,"Yes, everyone. Everyone."],[602.31,"Those people who are giving you trouble now are going to stop giving you trouble."],[606.39,"Yes. Sorry, pre trib folks."],[608.07,"Yes, when Christ returns, everyone's gonna see it."],[610.79,"Yeah. Yeah. As Christ said in the gospels, as lightning flashes from the east to the west, so will the coming of the Son of Man be."],[619.67,"Everyone's gonna know, everyone's going to see."],[622.87,"Verse 14 As obedient children this is continuing the sentence."],[633.17,"This is all part of girding up our loins and preparing ourselves for the struggles that we're gonna face in this life until Christ is manifest again."],[642.53,"As obedient children, not conforming yourselves to the former lusts, as in your ignorance, but as he who called you is holy, you also be holy in all your conduct, because it is written, be holy, for I am holy."],[661.93,"Um this is, you know, as I've said many times before, journal articles have to be published and dissertations have to be written."],[672.33,"Uh one of those topics is what is the makeup of these churches, these communities that St."],[680.09,"Peter is writing to?"],[681.21,"Are they Jewish or are they Gentile?"],[683.61,"Um you may have noticed I didn't talk much about that, other than that their connection to Rome is probably from the expulsion of the Jews."],[691.66,"Um the reason I didn't talk about that is because there's no way to know."],[696.62,"And exhibit A of that is this verse."],[699.46,"So, or these two verses."],[701.16,"So, verse 14, if we take that by itself, as obedient children, not conforming yourselves to the former lusts as in your ignorance, that makes it sound like they're Gentiles."],[710.42,"You were out there, you were ignorant, you didn't know any better, you were following your lusts and desires of the flesh."],[716.4,"Don't go back to that."],[718.05,"But then he says, But as he who called you is holy, you also be holy in all your conduct, because it is written, be holy, for I am holy, which is the phrase that's repeated over and over again in the Holiness Code in Leviticus."],[730.21,"So then he quotes the Torah."],[733.49,"So if you took that by itself, you'd think, oh, these must be Jewish people."],[738.38,"So there's no real way to isolate that."],[741.23,"Nor do we need to."],[743.87,"We talked about when we were talking about St."],[745.47,"James's Epistle, how there's this whole imaginary historical construct of sort of Jewish Christianity led by Saint James and Gentile Christianity led by St."],[756.51,"Paul, like there are these two different things"],[761.11,"And we talked about how that dog don't hunt, really."],[765.0,"So that doesn't that doesn't really work out."],[768.42,"And so, but part of that thinking is we have to try to isolate all this."],[772.98,"And so, as we talked about back when we were in the book of Acts, and we were talking about St."],[777.14,"Paul's missionary journeys and how he would go to the synagogue, and then he after he'd gone to the synagogue, he'd go to a wider audience."],[784.02,"Um most of Saint Paul's early Gentile converts did not convert directly from paganism to Christianity."],[795.22,"This is one of the big things that con that that that uh uh complicates that whole idea of there being Jewish Christianity and Gentile Christianity."],[805.98,"Most of them did not come straight from paganism, most of them were from this group that are referred to usually in the New Testament in Acts and elsewhere as God fearers."],[818.54,"So there were people who were non Jews."],[822.36,"And again, we're not talking about modern ethnicity, meaning you became a Jew by being circumcised if you were male, being the wife or daughter of a circumcised male if you're a woman, uh, and by eating the Passover."],[838.53,"That's the bare minimum that makes you a Jew."],[841.81,"So there were people who were not Jews, they had not been circumcised or were not married to or the Dharma person, and they weren't eating the Passover."],[850.89,"But who were sort of fascinated by Judaism."],[854.49,"In fact, there was kind of a Judaism fad uh in the first century BC and first century AD among Romans, not where they were gonna go convert."],[864.03,"There were no Roman men and Greek men lining up to get circumcised."],[867.61,"But they were fascinated by it for a bunch of reasons."],[871.21,"One reason was that Pompey, when he took uh annexed Jerusalem, uh famously barged into the Holy of Holies and found it empty."],[882.97,"And so he found it bizarre that there was no idol."],[886.67,"There's no image of the god there and everything."],[888.27,"Only with their minds"],[888.47,"And so he said, The Jews must worship their god solamente."],[894.37,"And so that sort of fascinated philosophically minded like Romans, like, wow, oh, this is some kind of refined religion."],[903.74,"This is some kind of so they're kind of fascinated with it."],[907.42,"They the uh Hebrew scriptures have been translated into Greek by this time and were in wide circulation in Greek."],[915.9,"In most of the world at synagogues, they were being read in Greek and only in Greek."],[922.3,"Uh so Gentiles, especially Gentiles of means, had access to them."],[927.5,"They could study them and learn about what they saw as this ancient and exotic religious tradition from the East."],[933.92,"Um, and we know that many of those people became benefactors of synagogues throughout the Roman world because we have like the dedication stones from these ancient Roman synagogues, and they have a bunch of names on them that are not Jewish names."],[952.14,"They are Latin and Greek names, and definitely pagan names."],[957.28,"Who were are being thanked for giving and endowing."],[960.42,"And this is this was a common part of Greek ethics going back to Aristotle, was that one of the virtues was being generous especially toward the gods."],[974.18,"So wealthy benefactors, this display of their piety, which was seen as a moral virtue, was endowing temples all over the place."],[984.63,"To all different gods."],[986.01,"Cover all your bases."],[987.23,"Make everybody like you."],[988.81,"Yeah, that would include synagogues."],[990.99,"Because they wouldn't take their money at the temple in Jerusalem."],[994.36,"But synagogues, that's how they kept, that's how they kept going."],[998.76,"Um, and so part of the dynamic that's playing out in the book of Acts was that, and the reason he faced so much resistance from the synagogues in these cities is he was coming to these godfearers, who kind of knew the Jewish scripture."],[1016.94,"Some of them would attend synagogue."],[1019.28,"Because they were kind of fascinated by it, and who were endowing and supporting these synagogues, and say, Hey, you could come and worship the God of Israel, you could become a Christian, and you don't have to get circumcised, and you don't have to worry about this other stuff."],[1036.01,"And so they would then become part of the Christian movement and maybe stop endowing the local synagogue."],[1043.19,"So there was a very material cluck conflict going on in a lot of these cities."],[1049.05,"Over this middle group that weren't pagans nor were they Jewish."],[1054.31,"This group in the middle were being sort of fought over."],[1057.81,"But most of St."],[1058.69,"Paul's Gentile converts were from that group."],[1062.73,"And so what that means is you can't make this clear cut distinction between a Gentile Christian and a Jewish Christian."],[1073.29,"It's more messy than that."],[1076.09,"And we've also talked about how diverse Judaism was at the time."],[1080.09,"So an Ethiopian Jew or a Jew in Alexandria or a Jew in Judea or Galilee, a Jew in Athens, a Jew in Rome, were not just culturally different, but they were reading different Jewish texts."],[1096.65,"They had very different views on different things."],[1099.05,"There were all these different Jewish sects."],[1101.05,"So even if you're just talking about quote unquote Jewish Christianity, that's not like a monolithic thing either."],[1106.85,"So there's a lot of diversity here in different people and groups."],[1110.9,"And so all of these commun, almost all of these communities."],[1115.54,"I mean, the community in Jerusalem was obviously very much Jewish."],[1120.98,"You go to a place like Athens, and it's very clear in Acts that most of Paul's converts, based on their names, were pagans."],[1127.32,"We talked about in Corinth, most of them were pagans."],[1130.28,"But that's depending on the city and the size of the Jewish community there and right so and the makeup."],[1136.12,"But they're all this mixed makeup of different people from different backgrounds and different former beliefs."],[1142.44,"And so St."],[1143.16,"Peter is writing to all of them."],[1145.46,"And it's kind of a mistake to do what's called mirror reading and try to figure out based on what he says who the audience is right and not only is it sort of problematic in terms of trying to determine it but you end up creating this kind of weird interpretive circle where you're like okay based on this he's talking to this group and since he's talking to this group he must mean this now you've created this circle that's based on nothing right it's just this sort of recursive interpretive loop."],[1181.1,"Yes. But don't you think also that his references to script to old testament to scripture would not it would include Gentiles."],[1197.14,"It would it would include the sense that that continuity of faith and a you know kind of breaking open of revelation, it it doesn't seem that old test the old testament would be um only for the Jews."],[1216.14,"That's that's yeah, that's part of what I'm aiming at."],[1218.38,"Okay. So he could he can quote the fact that he's quoting Leviticus doesn't mean he's only talking to Jews."],[1227.23,"Yeah. Yeah. The word for, it just seems that it's more of a reference and context."],[1232.99,"Yeah, and he can."],[1234.63,"And likewise he, can say. to someone who was previously in the Jewish synagogue, don't go back to your former lusts"],[1244.45,"Because it's not like every Jewish person in the world was totally perfect in terms of the Torah before before they they joined the Christian movement."],[1253.87,"So that's what yeah, so that's a supporting point."],[1258.52,"That that it's not that neat."],[1260.46,"It's not that, you know."],[1266.7,"Okay. But so um the fact that he's quoting this part of Leviticus, though, is also important."],[1274.26,"Cause this is gonna be a sorry we're I'm gonna be picking on a different group of people in this book."],[1278.7,"Instead of sorry, Calvinists it's gonna be sorry, dispensationalists"],[1282.34,"Um the holiness code in Leviticus is Leviticus usually it's marked off as Leviticus Leviticus chapters 17 through 23."],[1294.74,"And this be holy for I am holy is a refrain through it, and that phrase that St."],[1299.46,"Peter quotes is why it's called the Holiness Code."],[1302.47,"Uh as a literary unit."],[1304.07,"And and this includes a lot of the laws."],[1307.27,"This includes like the food laws."],[1308.95,"This includes this is mostly directed at Israel and specifically at Israel."],[1317.51,"And when I say specifically at Israel, I mean Israel was never called to go and enforce the these parts of their law on neighboring nations."],[1328.53,"They didn't say you need to go conquer Moab and don't let them eat pork anymore."],[1334.74,"There was nothing like that."],[1336.71,"These aren't things that they were to enforce other people."],[1340.47,"And in the Holiness Code, as we talked about, and when we talked about Acts 15, way back in the long ago time, there were only four commandments, the four commandments that end up in Acts 15 that were even applied to Gentiles living in Israel."],[1358.13,"So most of the Holiness Code, most of this body of text that St."],[1362.45,"Peter is citing here, applied only to the nation of Israel."],[1366.29,"They were bound to these laws"],[1367.05,"Only to people who were circumcised, ate the Passover, and did all these things."],[1373.11,"And Saint Peter is now applying these texts to Christian churches, Christian communities."],[1384.04,"Meaning he thinks that the latter is a continuation of the former, not a different thing than the former."],[1394.97,"That these Christian communities are a continuation of the synagogues in those places."],[1402.25,"Not that, you know, well, that's over and now we've got this new thing."],[1406.26,"That's different, has a different set of rules."],[1407.94,"And none none of that Torah stuff, of that law stuff applies to it anymore."],[1408.26,"of that Remember, we talked about this is one the ways people try to set St."],[1408.5,"Peter and St"],[1415.9,"James against St."],[1417.1,"Paul. Oh well, St."],[1418.62,"Paul was against all that Torah stuff."],[1421.02,"St. James was liked all that Torah stuff."],[1423.6,"As we saw that Dunwork."],[1425.7,"Because St. Paul says all kinds of good things about the Torah."],[1429.38,"Then so does St."],[1430.66,"James. But St."],[1432.04,"James is also not some kind of weird legalist."],[1435.66,"As we saw."],[1436.63,"Any more than St."],[1437.51,"Paul is. So, but so he's applying it here."],[1442.85,"And this is, notice he says, be holy in all your conduct."],[1447.77,"That yours added."],[1449.05,"In all conduct."],[1449.95,"But he's directing it to you."],[1450.99,"Okay It's an imperative."],[1453.59,"And so this again is pointing to the Torah."],[1459.11,"Why? Because he's not just saying, hey, don't break the Ten Commandments."],[1464.81,"This is a higher thing than that."],[1466.73,"This is in all your ways."],[1468.79,"And we talk about how Torah means teaching."],[1473.36,"And it's a lot, I won't go through it all now, but we talk about how there's this weird linguistic translation process where we end up receiving it as law."],[1480.56,"But that kind of distorts what it is."],[1482.64,"The idea of teaching, and in Greek gnomos, includes the idea of culture, uh, morality, ethics, even politics, when you talk about the ethics within a community."],[1493.98,"ic The ancient conception of polits."],[1494.06,"s. All that' included in that idea"],[1499.98,"So it's saying Peter says, in all your conduct be holy."],[1504.48,"This isn't just a matter of not violating some rules."],[1507.77,"This is a positive command, and this embraces all aspects of life, sort of the way the Torah does."],[1519.74,"Verse 17, and if you call on the Father, who without partiality judges according to each one's work, conduct yourselves throughout the time of your stay here in fear."],[1535.18,"Um calling on the Father."],[1538.6,"Remember the Old Testament, the phrase calling on the name of the Lord, was used for worship."],[1546.59,"Someone would build an altar and would say, He built an altar, and there he called upon the name of the Lord."],[1552.04,"That doesn't mean he was just yelling God's name."],[1554.46,"That means he was worshiping, he was offering sacrifices, he was offering worship."],[1558.64,"So he's saying, If you if you truly worship the father, if you truly worship God the Father, the one who judges without partiality each person according to their work."],[1572.43,"If you worship him and you know that he's going to judge you according to your works, then you should. be conducting yourself throughout the time of your stay here, throughout this life, in fear"],[1583.81,"And that's not fear like being terrified of God."],[1588.29,"That's fear is in awe and reverence."],[1591.19,"Meaning you have in mind, the day is going to come when I'm going to stand before God and have to give an account."],[1597.07,"And so if I really believe in him, if I really am worshiping, seeking to follow God, then the way I will show that is by conducting myself in a way that reflects the fact that I'm going to stand in front of him."],[1613.88,"One day and give an account for this."],[1615.44,"So not solo feeding."],[1617.28,"Well, I would, you know, I'm trying to restrict who I bash. to just one group at a time in a given night"],[1625.88,"But yes, it does say he's going to judge us according to our works."],[1631.8,"Verse 18, knowing that you are not redeemed with corruptible things like silver or gold from your aimless conduct received by tradition from your fathers."],[1644.6,"Well, we'll pause there."],[1647.32,"But I'll make this short."],[1648.6,"So we weren't redeemed with corruptible things like silver and gold."],[1652.36,"Notice he says corruptible things, and then it says, like silver and gold."],[1657.16,"So in the minds of ancient people, silver and gold were not corruptible things."],[1664.28,"Corruptible things were things. that you would buy with silver and gold"],[1669.58,"And so if you wanted to hold on to your wealth, you didn't go and buy clothes and food and things that rot and rust and go away."],[1678.96,"You'd get silver and gold because you could lay it up."],[1681.78,"Forever. Nothing's gonna happen to it."],[1684.63,"So when he says with corruptible things like silver and gold, St."],[1690.0,"Peter is making the point that even those things that you think are the most reliable and the most trustworthy."],[1697.32,"When you take into account the shortness of our life."],[1700.26,"Our stay here, they're ultimately just as corruptible."],[1706.16,"In the end. result"],[1709.01,"Um that's not what we were redeemed with."],[1713.27,"Because of course, redeemed here is the idea of redemption is buying back."],[1717.95,"So God didn't redeem us from slavery to sin with money."],[1724.67,"He didn't like just go buy us out of slavery with money."],[1728.19,"And that's from your aimless conduct."],[1729.79,"Slaver for sin is the aimless conduct received by tradition from your fathers."],[1735.63,"So, just briefly again, there are people who will look at this and say, Oh, well, this must be Gentiles."],[1743.89,"Because, say, Peter wouldn't call the Torah aimless conduct received by tradition from your fathers."],[1753.5,"But he could also be referring to the fact that if they're Jewish, their fathers in the Old Testament didn't keep the Torah."],[1761.1,"And he could be saying, You're just like see what I mean about it being kind of a waste of time trying to figure out who's who in the audience."],[1769.04,"But so the point is because of that conduct."],[1773.25,"Because of that conduct, we we were made slaves, to sin."],[1778.0,"And this isn't just something we came up with, like this for generations, we've been enslaved to sin."],[1785.38,"And now God has redeemed us from that."],[1788.2,"And he didn't redeem us from that with any material things."],[1793.22,"Verse 19, but with the precious blood of Christ, as of a lamb without blemish and without spot."],[1802.5,"Okay. So it was the blood of Christ that rescued us, which is far more precious, obviously, than even gold and silver, which in the end result are just material things."],[1817.38,"It's as of a lamb without blemish and without spot."],[1820.02,"Why is that important?"],[1821.38,"Well, where does a lamb without blemish and without spot show up in the Old Testament?"],[1826.98,"Passover. Passover. And what happens with the blood of the Passover lamb?"],[1835.62,"We jump too quickly because we've gotten this Western view of atonement in our head."],[1836.02,"ed. So Christ was kill"],[1840.94,"We think, oh, the blood of Christ."],[1843.82,"That's what did it."],[1845.22,"Well, you can go look at the Passover ritual."],[1848.84,"It's not ritualized how you kill the lamb."],[1851.24,"You have to kill it because you're going to cook it and eat it."],[1853.26,"So you're not going to eat it alive."],[1856.19,"So, and you have to get the blood out."],[1858.69,"So, because you're going to do something else with the blood, and then you're going to eat it."],[1862.02,"And there's not even an element of substitution."],[1865.86,"It's been a while since we talked about this, so I'll briefly go over it again with that lamb."],[1870.16,"So, if there was an element of substitution, and some people, again, who have that Western idea of atonement in their head, will present the Passover this way."],[1879.0,"They'll say, okay, God's killing all the firstborn in Egypt."],[1883.32,"For some reason, he wants to also kill all the firstborn Israelites."],[1889.72,"Now I don't know why that would be, first of all."],[1893.18,"Because the reason he was killing the firstborn of the Egyptians was that the Egyptians murdered the Israelite babies."],[1900.32,"So this is about balancing the scales of justice."],[1903.68,"They murdered the Israelite children who God identified as his children."],[1909.28,"So they murdered God's children."],[1912.66,"And so God is now going to take their firstborn children, is the idea."],[1916.5,"So I don't know why he would want to kill more Israelite children."],[1920.1,"But it's posited that he did"],[1920.3,"That alone doesn't make sense."],[1925.42,"But he would accept, or he would be sated, I guess."],[1930.78,"And his desire to kill a child would be sated if someone killed a lamb instead, provided it was a pure lamb without spot or blemish."],[1945.66,"That they're killing the lamb instead of the kid."],[1947.99,"So when the Israelites are told to do this, what about families that don't have any sons?"],[1956.23,"They just not have to do it."],[1958.79,"Well, no, they're still supposed to do it."],[1962.44,"What about a family where both the father and his son are firstborn sons?"],[1969.84,"Did they do two lambs?"],[1972.72,"No, they just do one."],[1975.36,"And in fact, if you read Exodus closely, if you have a really small family and there's another small family next door, and you're not going to be able to eat a whole lamb by yourself, you can share one with your neighbor."],[1989.04,"So is it that in that case God will take one lamb for two babies?"],[1994.34,"It doesn't make sense."],[1995.72,"It doesn't map out."],[1998.33,"But if you read the text in Exodus, uh, I believe it's Exodus 13, if I'm remembering correctly."],[2006.61,"If you read that, God says what he's doing, and it's not that."],[2010.93,"He says, Tonight I will make a distinction between the Israelites and the Egyptians."],[2018.05,"That's the purpose of the Passover ritual."],[2020.69,"To make a distinction between the Israelites and the Egyptians."],[2023.41,"Because the previous plagues, many of them at least, fell on the areas where of Egypt, other than the place where most of the Israelites lived."],[2034.05,"But if you were an Egyptian living in that neighborhood, you lucked out because they didn't fall on you."],[2039.93,"So it was just based on the rough area where people were living."],[2043.57,"Okay, but on that night, God says, I'm making a distinction."],[2047.37,"So the part of the ritual that involves the blood that St."],[2050.65,"blood. Peter is referring to here was that that was put on the doorpost"],[2057.25,"Remember? And when the blood was put on the doorpost, Angel of Death passed by."],[2064.79,"Angel of Death passed by."],[2067.35,"So on that night, everyone who marked their doorpost with the blood of a lamb like that was an Israelite from that day forward."],[2076.47,"It didn't matter ethnically where they were from."],[2079.53,"We know later on some of them were black Africans, some of them were Egyptians."],[2084.09,"I mean, it says so in the text."],[2085.45,"Some of them were Canaanites."],[2087.39,"But they were all Israelites if they did that."],[2090.83,"And anyone who didn't do that on that night in Egypt."],[2096.31,"Didn't matter if they were directly descended from Abraham and could trace their lineage back."],[2100.87,"If they did not mark the door, they were now an Egyptian."],[2105.45,"So this was the purpose."],[2107.3,"Was to make this distinction and to mark to mark these people out."],[2113.38,"So this is what Saint Peter is comparing our redemption to."],[2119.2,"And redemption's the appropriate word because Passover is about manumission."],[2123.92,"And I use that word just because I want to teach everyone the word manumission, which means to purchase out of slavery."],[2131.84,"So that's what happens."],[2133.46,"A bunch of people who are slaves in Egypt, through the Passover are set free."],[2140.61,"And so this is the comparison St."],[2143.09,"Peter is making."],[2145.33,"So we were enslaved to sin."],[2147.89,"And the wages of sin is death, as St."],[2151.25,"Paul told us."],[2152.77,"So that's where we were headed."],[2154.69,"We were headed for death."],[2158.09,"But Christ offered himself, and we've been now marked out with the blood of Christ."],[2163.61,"And so we escape death."],[2166.81,"And are freed from slavery to sin."],[2170.03,"That's what St."],[2170.75,"Peter's getting at."],[2174.56,"But it was the blood of Christ that did that."],[2176.88,"It was Christ who did that for us."],[2178.8,"And that's far more precious than gold and silver and all the other things this world has to offer"],[2185.14,"Because what's the point St."],[2186.26,"Peter's been making here?"],[2187.86,"How did this, again, context?"],[2190.9,"First verse we read."],[2192.9,"Put all your hope on the grace of Christ at his appearing."],[2196.98,"Which means put none of your hope where?"],[2199.62,"Gold, silver, the things of this world."],[2204.32,"Why? Because those aren't the things that saved you from death."],[2208.72,"They're not gonna save you from death."],[2211.04,"It's Christ who saved us from sin and death."],[2218.64,"Verse 20. He indeed was foreordained before the foundation of the world, but was manifest in these last times for you."],[2229.64,"So notice the language there."],[2231.64,"We pointed this out a lot in St."],[2233.48,"Paul. St. Peter is pretty clear that Christ preexisted his birth as a man."],[2244.7,"Because he was foreigned for the foundation of the world, and he refers to him being born from the Theotogos as him being manifest on earth."],[2255.52,"What is he comparing that to?"],[2257.28,"The revelation of Christ at the end."],[2260.08,"When he'll be manifest again."],[2267.93,"Verse 21, who through him, that being the you, you who through him, I'll retranslate it, are faithful to God, who raised him from the dead and gave him glory, so that your faith and hope are in God."],[2285.14,"God the Father raised him from the dead and gave him glory."],[2289.72,"And therefore your faithfulness in this life and your hope in the next life are in God."],[2296.36,"Why? That God will do the same for you, will raise you from the dead and bring you into his glory, as he did for Christ."],[2307.72,"Verse 22 Since you have purified your souls in obeying the truth through the Spirit, in sincere love of the brethren, love one another fervently with a pure heart."],[2324.72,"So he's still, we're still after the therefore."],[2328.96,"So the one thing is this preparation, focusing on Christ."],[2334.93,"And then secondly, that was sort of directed toward God."],[2338.17,"Our faithfulness toward God, our hope in God."],[2341.21,"Now it's talking about other people, the other people in the Christian community."],[2345.73,"And you talk about being holy, so if we've purified our souls and obeying the truth."],[2351.63,"If we've purified our souls before God, then now we also love one another."],[2359.63,"And live in love with each other with a pure heart."],[2364.11,"Verse 23, having been born again, not of corruptible seed, but incorruptible, through the word of God which lives and abides forever."],[2372.91,"The word of God should be capitalized there, because obviously he's not talking about the Bible."],[2376.83,"Bible didn't exist."],[2384.01,"Who lives and abides forever?"],[2386.17,"As he just said from the foundation of the world."],[2391.58,"And so he's this is another way of coming at."],[2394.14,"Being being preserved from death"],[2395.32,"He talked about being set free from slavery to sin."],[2400.97,"And now he's talking about being born again."],[2403.71,"Not literally. Again, he's contrasting that same contrast."],[2408.11,"The things of this world are corruptible."],[2411.21,"All of our human fathers were imperfect men."],[2415.74,"Even the nicest and the greatest human fathers are not perfect."],[2420.64,"And some are very, very not perfect."],[2423.33,"But they're all they're all corruptible."],[2426.13,"And to some extent corrupted and struggling with sin."],[2430.35,"But God, our Father, is incorruptible."],[2434.09,"And is not"],[2437.47,"And so now we've been born again as his children."],[2442.17,"And so this is a life where we can love one another with a pure heart, as opposed to the sort of corrupted forms of love and lusts and whatever else we lived in before."],[2453.69,"As he said."],[2456.81,"Verse 24 Because all flesh is as grass, and all the glory of man is the flower of the grass, the grass withers and its flower falls away, but the word of the Lord endures forever."],[2471.53,"That's from Isaiah chapter 40."],[2474.69,"Um, so this makes it pretty clear."],[2477.46,"He draws on this analogy from the prophet Isaiah."],[2477.78,"All flesh, our lives in this world is like the grass"],[2483.8,"It's here today, it's dried up and gone tomorrow."],[2487.18,"It withers, flowers fall away."],[2490.32,"But the word of the Lord, capital W, again, Christ, endures forever."],[2499.94,"Now, this is the word, capital W, which by the gospel was preached to you."],[2505.94,"The gospel preached Christ to them, it didn't preach the Bible to them."],[2512.06,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2519.34,"Father Stephen.'s email address is wholecouncil at ancient faith.com"],[2524.74,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.56,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: 1 Peter 1:1-12 and the turn","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>1 Peter 1:13</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Last time: <b>intro to 1 Peter, 2 Peter, Jude</b> - why those, and why <b>Jude taken out of order</b>; won't rehearse</li><li class=\"d0\">Read first <b>12 verses</b> = intro to the epistle</li><li class=\"d1\">format very like <b>St. Paul's epistles</b> (read over a couple of years)</li><li class=\"d1\">intro lays out <b>themes</b> he'll keep returning to</li><li class=\"d0\">Audience: <b>Roman colonies started by Claudius</b> - same Claudius who <b>expelled the Jews from Rome</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Peter writes <b>from Rome</b> → likely addressed to some of those <b>exiles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Uses <b>diaspora / exile language</b>; they face <b>hardship</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Against hardship: <b>Christ as example</b> - beyond suffering, <b>hope</b></li><li class=\"d1\">beyond Christ's death, <b>resurrection</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Intro ended on <b>Christ's second coming</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now the shift: <b>\"therefore\"</b> - what you need to <b>do</b> in light of this</li><li class=\"d1\">same rhetorical move as <b>St. Paul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Reports from foreign markets?\" - none, on to v. 13</li></ul>"},{"t":237.6,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 1:13 - Gird up the loins of your mind","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v. 13: \"gird up the loins of your mind, be sober, rest your hope fully on the grace to be brought at the <b>revelation of Jesus Christ</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"I didn't know my mind had loins\" - phrase occurs <b>several times in OT</b></li><li class=\"d1\">used <b>metaphorically</b> here, but refers to a <b>literal thing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Literal: long <b>tunic / robe folded upward and tucked</b></li><li class=\"d1\">for <b>battle</b>, or running from battle</li><li class=\"d1\">\"don't Google anything with <i>loins</i> in it\" - diagrams exist of how they folded it</li><li class=\"d1\">→ <b>freedom of movement</b></li><li class=\"d0\">= <b>preparing yourself</b> for battle, struggle, contest, adversity</li><li class=\"d1\">like \"<b>prepping</b>\" in our language - but of your <b>mind</b></li><li class=\"d1\">battle is <b>spiritual</b>, not physical - \"not <b>revolution against Roman overlords</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">= get ready for <b>what lies ahead</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sober</b> = not just not drinking</li><li class=\"d1\">being <b>serious, focused, undistracted</b>, not dulled</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Rest hope fully</b> on grace at Christ's revelation</li></ul>"},{"t":381.97,"lv":3,"h":"Not \"second coming\": revelation and <i>parousia</i>","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Our vocabulary: \"<b>second coming</b>\" - he's said it himself tonight</li><li class=\"d1\">implies at the Ascension he <b>went up into the sky</b>, went away somewhere, will <b>come back</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"obviously not what we actually believe\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Every liturgy: <b>\"Christ is in our midst\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">based on promise: <b>\"wherever two or three are gathered in my name, I will be there\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ not <b>off somewhere</b> in the meantime</li><li class=\"d0\">NT words actually used</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: Christ's <b>glorious appearing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">here: <b>revelation</b> - he's <b>veiled</b> from us now, will be <b>revealed</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ he's here, we just can't see him <b>tactilely, physically, materially</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Greek <i>parousia</i> usually rendered \"second coming\" - <b>doesn't really mean that</b></li><li class=\"d1\">para + <i>ousia</i> (= being) → <b>presence</b>, being present</li><li class=\"d1\">someone becoming present → <b>arrival</b> → \"coming\" in English</li><li class=\"d1\">but also <b>appearing, manifesting</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ will become present <b>in a different way than now</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Peter won't tell sufferers \"Christ is <b>far away</b>, but he's coming back\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not true</b>, and not reassuring for hope</li><li class=\"d1\">instead: he's here, and the day is coming when his presence is <b>manifest to the whole world</b></li></ul>"},{"t":580.19,"lv":3,"h":"Aside: everyone will see it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: isn't \"revelation\" a good word for it?</li><li class=\"d1\">yes - what Peter uses; <b>glorious appearing</b> (Paul), <b>revelation</b>, <i>parousia</i> = arrival / appearance</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Everyone</b> will see it - \"sorry, <b>pre-trib folks</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ: \"as <b>lightning flashes from east to west</b>, so will the coming of the Son of Man be\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"everyone's gonna know, everyone's going to see\"</li></ul>"},{"t":622.87,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 1:14-16 - Be holy as he is holy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v. 14-16: \"as <b>obedient children</b>, not conforming to the <b>former lusts</b> as in your <b>ignorance</b>, but as he who called you is <b>holy</b>, be holy in all your conduct, because it is written <b>'be holy for I am holy'</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Still <b>one sentence</b> - part of girding up and preparing for struggles <b>until Christ is manifest again</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Scholars' topic: makeup of Peter's churches - <b>Jewish or Gentile?</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he didn't dwell on it: <b>\"no way to know\"</b>; exhibit A = <b>this verse</b></li><li class=\"d1\">v. 14 alone: \"ignorance\", \"lusts\" → sounds <b>Gentile</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"be holy\" = <b>Torah quote</b>, refrain of the <b>holiness code in Leviticus</b> → sounds <b>Jewish</b></li><li class=\"d0\">No real way to isolate - <b>nor do we need to</b></li><li class=\"d1\">same as James: imaginary <b>Jewish Christianity (James) vs Gentile Christianity (Paul)</b> - \"<b>that dog don't hunt</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">that thinking assumes we must <b>isolate all this</b></li></ul>"},{"t":772.98,"lv":3,"h":"Paul's Gentile converts were God-fearers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Recall Acts: Paul goes to the <b>synagogue</b> first, then wider audience</li><li class=\"d0\">Most early Gentile converts <b>did not come straight from paganism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they were <b>God-fearers</b> - non-Jews fascinated by Judaism</li><li class=\"d1\">complicates the Jewish / Gentile Christianity idea</li><li class=\"d0\">Who counts as a Jew (not modern ethnicity): <b>circumcised male</b>, <b>wife or daughter</b> of one, and <b>eats the Passover</b> - bare minimum</li><li class=\"d0\">God-fearers: not circumcised, not married to one, not eating Passover</li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>Judaism fad</b> among Romans, 1st c. BC - 1st c. AD</li><li class=\"d1\">no Roman men \"lining up to get circumcised\"</li><li class=\"d1\">fascination 1: <b>Pompey</b> annexed Jerusalem, barged into the <b>Holy of Holies</b> - <b>empty</b>, no idol</li><li class=\"d1\">→ \"Jews worship their god <b>only with their minds</b>\" → refined religion to <b>philosophical Romans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fascination 2: <b>Hebrew scriptures in Greek</b>, wide circulation; read in synagogues <b>in Greek only</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ Gentiles \"<b>of means</b>\" could study this \"ancient and exotic religious tradition from the East\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Many became <b>benefactors of synagogues</b></li><li class=\"d1\">dedication stones with <b>Latin and Greek names</b>, \"definitely pagan\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Greek ethics back to <b>Aristotle</b>: <b>generosity toward the gods</b> a virtue</li><li class=\"d1\">endowed temples to all gods - \"<b>cover all your bases</b>\" - synagogues too; not taken at the Jerusalem temple</li></ul>"},{"t":998.76,"lv":3,"h":"Fight over the middle group","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Acts dynamic: why Paul met so much <b>resistance from synagogues</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he'd tell God-fearers: <b>worship the God of Israel, become Christian, no circumcision</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ they might <b>stop endowing the synagogue</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>very material conflict</b> over this middle group - neither pagan nor Jewish</li><li class=\"d0\">Most of Paul's Gentile converts came from that group</li><li class=\"d0\">→ <b>no clear-cut line</b> between Gentile and Jewish Christian - \"more messy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Judaism itself <b>diverse</b>: Ethiopian, Alexandrian, Judean, Galilean, Athenian, Roman Jews</li><li class=\"d1\">not just cultural - read <b>different Jewish texts</b>, different views, <b>different sects</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Jewish Christianity\" not <b>monolithic</b> either</li></ul>"},{"t":1110.9,"lv":3,"h":"Mixed churches; don't mirror-read","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Jerusalem: <b>very much Jewish</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Athens: converts mostly <b>pagans</b> by name (Acts); Corinth: mostly pagans</li><li class=\"d1\">depends on <b>city, size of Jewish community, makeup</b></li><li class=\"d0\">All <b>mixed</b> - different backgrounds, former beliefs</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter writing to <b>all of them</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Mistake: <b>mirror reading</b> - deducing audience from what he says</li><li class=\"d1\">problematic, and creates a <b>weird interpretive circle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"based on this he's talking to this group; so he must mean this\" → <b>recursive loop</b> \"based on nothing\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1181.1,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: OT quotes and Gentiles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: don't his OT references include Gentiles - <b>continuity of faith</b>, revelation \"breaking open\"; OT not just for Jews?</li><li class=\"d1\">yes - part of what he's aiming at: quoting <b>Leviticus</b> ≠ only talking to Jews</li><li class=\"d0\">Likewise \"don't go back to your <b>former lusts</b>\" can go to someone <b>formerly in the synagogue</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not every Jewish person was <b>perfect in Torah</b> before joining the Christian movement</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not that neat\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1266.7,"lv":2,"h":"Holiness Code applied to the church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Quoting this part of Leviticus matters - \"sorry, <b>dispensationalists</b>\" (next group picked on)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Holiness Code</b> = Lev <b>17-23</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>be holy for I am holy</b>\" = refrain → why it's called that; a literary unit</li><li class=\"d1\">includes <b>food laws</b>; mostly directed <b>specifically at Israel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Israel never called to <b>enforce</b> these on neighbors</li><li class=\"d1\">\"go conquer <b>Moab</b> and don't let them eat pork\" - nothing like that</li><li class=\"d1\">only <b>four commandments</b> (those in <b>Acts 15</b>) applied even to <b>Gentiles living in Israel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Most of it bound only <b>circumcised, Passover-eating Israel</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Peter now applies it to <b>Christian communities</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ the latter is a <b>continuation</b> of the former, not a different thing</li><li class=\"d1\">churches = continuation of <b>the synagogues</b> there - not \"that's over, new thing, <b>no Torah stuff</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same move people use to pit <b>Peter and James against Paul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul says <b>good things about Torah</b>; so does James</li><li class=\"d1\">James no \"weird <b>legalist</b>\", any more than Paul</li></ul>"},{"t":1442.85,"lv":3,"h":"\"In all your conduct\": Torah as teaching","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Be holy in <b>all conduct</b>\" - not \"<b>yours</b>\" (added); directed to you, an <b>imperative</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Again pointing to <b>Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just \"don't break the <b>Ten Commandments</b>\" - <b>higher</b> than that: \"in all your ways\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Torah = teaching</b></li><li class=\"d1\">weird translation process → received as <b>law</b>, which <b>distorts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Greek <i>nomos</i> includes <b>culture, morality, ethics, even politics</b> (ancient sense: ethics within a community)</li><li class=\"d0\">So holiness = not just avoiding violated rules</li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>positive command</b> embracing <b>all aspects of life</b>, like Torah</li></ul>"},{"t":1519.74,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 1:17 - The Father judges impartially","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v. 17: \"if you call on the <b>Father</b>, who <b>without partiality</b> judges according to each one's <b>work</b>, conduct yourselves throughout the time of your <b>stay here</b> in <b>fear</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Calling on the name of the Lord</b> in OT = <b>worship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"built an altar, and there he called upon the name of the Lord\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not just yelling God's name - <b>offering sacrifices, worship</b></li><li class=\"d0\">= if you truly <b>worship God the Father</b>, who judges each by works</li><li class=\"d1\">→ conduct yourself <b>throughout this life</b> in fear</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Fear</b> not terror, but <b>awe and reverence</b></li><li class=\"d1\">keep in mind: <b>I will stand before God and give an account</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if I really believe, seek to follow → live in a way that <b>reflects that</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Yes - he does say he'll <b>judge us according to our works</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1631.8,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 1:18 - Not redeemed with silver and gold","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v. 18: \"not redeemed with <b>corruptible things</b> like silver or gold from your <b>aimless conduct</b> received by <b>tradition from your fathers</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Odd: silver and gold as \"corruptible\"?</li><li class=\"d1\">ancients: they were <b>not</b> corruptible - corruptible = what you <b>buy with</b> them (clothes, food: <b>rot and rust</b>)</li><li class=\"d1\">to keep wealth you bought <b>silver and gold</b> - \"nothing's gonna happen to it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter's point: even what seems <b>most reliable</b>, given the <b>shortness of our life</b>, is <b>just as corruptible</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Redemption</b> = <b>buying back</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God didn't buy us out of slavery to sin <b>with money</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Aimless conduct</b> = slavery to sin, \"received by tradition from your fathers\"</li><li class=\"d1\">some say <b>must be Gentiles</b> - Peter wouldn't call Torah \"aimless\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but if Jewish, their <b>fathers in the OT didn't keep Torah</b> - again, a waste of time sorting <b>who's who</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Because of that conduct we were <b>made slaves to sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not our invention - <b>generations enslaved</b>; God redeemed us <b>not with material things</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1793.22,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 1:19 - The blood of Christ, a lamb without blemish","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v. 19: \"but with the <b>precious blood of Christ</b>, as of a <b>lamb without blemish and without spot</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Blood of Christ rescued us - <b>far more precious</b> than gold and silver, \"just material things\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Lamb without blemish or spot → OT: <b>Passover</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We jump too quickly - <b>Western view of atonement</b> in our heads</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Christ was killed, <b>that's what did it</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Look at the <b>Passover ritual</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not ritualized</b> how you kill the lamb - you kill it because you'll <b>cook and eat</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">blood drained because you'll <b>do something else with the blood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no <b>substitution</b> element</li></ul>"},{"t":1870.16,"lv":3,"h":"Why substitution doesn't fit Passover","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Westerners' version: God kills <b>firstborn of Egypt</b>, \"for some reason\" also firstborn Israelites</li><li class=\"d1\">but Egyptians' firstborn killed because Egypt <b>murdered Israelite babies</b> - <b>balancing the scales of justice</b></li><li class=\"d1\">they murdered <b>God's children</b>, so he takes theirs</li><li class=\"d1\">why would he want to kill <b>more Israelite children</b>? Doesn't make sense</li><li class=\"d1\">idea: his desire to kill a child <b>sated</b> by a <b>pure lamb</b> instead</li><li class=\"d0\">Problems with lamb-for-kid</li><li class=\"d1\">families with <b>no sons</b>? Still supposed to do it</li><li class=\"d1\">father and son both <b>firstborn</b>? Still <b>one lamb</b>, not two</li><li class=\"d1\">small family can <b>share a lamb with the neighbor</b> (Exodus) → one lamb for two babies?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"doesn't map out\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1998.33,"lv":3,"h":"Passover: a distinction marked by blood","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Exodus 13</b> (\"if I'm remembering correctly\"): God says what he's doing - <b>not that</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"Tonight I will make a <b>distinction between the Israelites and the Egyptians</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">= purpose of the Passover ritual</li><li class=\"d0\">Earlier plagues fell on the <b>area</b> where Israelites lived - Egyptian in that neighborhood \"<b>lucked out</b>\"; rough geography</li><li class=\"d0\">That night: <b>blood on the doorpost</b> → <b>Angel of Death passed by</b></li><li class=\"d1\">anyone who marked the doorpost = an <b>Israelite from that day forward</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ethnicity irrelevant - some <b>black Africans</b>, <b>Egyptians</b>, <b>Canaanites</b> (\"says so in the text\")</li><li class=\"d1\">anyone who didn't mark it = <b>Egyptian</b>, even if <b>descended from Abraham</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Point: <b>mark out</b> a people</li></ul>"},{"t":2113.38,"lv":3,"h":"Passover as our redemption (manumission)","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">What Peter compares our redemption to</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Passover</b> is about <b>manumission</b> = purchase out of slavery (\"want to teach everyone the word\")</li><li class=\"d1\">slaves in Egypt set free through the Passover</li><li class=\"d0\">Comparison</li><li class=\"d1\">we were <b>enslaved to sin</b>; <b>wages of sin is death</b> (Paul) → headed for <b>death</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ offered himself; we're <b>marked with the blood of Christ</b> → <b>escape death</b>, <b>freed from slavery to sin</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Far more precious than gold, silver, \"all the things this world has to offer\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Peter's context, <b>first verse</b>: <b>hope on the grace of Christ at his appearing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ <b>none of your hope</b> in gold, silver, this world</li><li class=\"d1\">they didn't and won't save from death - <b>Christ saved us from sin and death</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2218.64,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 1:20 - Foreordained, manifest in the last times","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v. 20: \"foreordained <b>before the foundation of the world</b>, but <b>manifest</b> in these last times for you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same language pointed out in <b>Paul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Peter clear: Christ <b>pre-existed his birth as a man</b></li><li class=\"d1\">foreordained before the world's foundation</li><li class=\"d1\">birth from the <b>Theotokos</b> = <b>manifest on earth</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Compared to: the revelation at the end, when he's <b>manifest again</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2267.93,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 1:21 - Faith and hope in God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v. 21: \"who <b>through him</b> are faithful to God, who <b>raised him from the dead and gave him glory</b>, so that your <b>faith and hope</b> are in God\" (his retranslation)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>God the Father</b> raised him and gave him glory</li><li class=\"d1\">→ <b>faithfulness in this life</b>, <b>hope in the next</b> are in God</li><li class=\"d0\">Why? God will do the <b>same for you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">raise you and bring you into <b>his glory</b>, as for Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":2307.72,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 1:22 - Love one another fervently","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v. 22: \"since you have <b>purified your souls</b> in obeying the truth through the Spirit, in <b>sincere love of the brethren</b>, love one another <b>fervently with a pure heart</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Still after the <b>\"therefore\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first: <b>preparation, focusing on Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">second: directed toward <b>God</b> - faithfulness, hope</li><li class=\"d1\">now: <b>other people</b> in the Christian community</li><li class=\"d0\">Tied to <b>being holy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">purified souls before God → now <b>love one another</b>, live in love <b>with a pure heart</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2364.11,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 1:23 - Born again of incorruptible seed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v. 23: \"<b>born again</b>, not of corruptible seed but <b>incorruptible</b>, through the <b>word of God</b> which lives and abides forever\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Word of God</b> should be capitalized - \"obviously <b>not the Bible</b>\" - it didn't exist</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>Christ</b>, who lives and abides forever, as he just said <b>from the foundation of the world</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Another angle: set free from <b>slavery to sin</b>, <b>preserved from death</b>, now <b>born again</b> - \"not literally\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same contrast: world's things <b>corruptible</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all <b>human fathers imperfect</b> - even the greatest; some \"very, very not perfect\"</li><li class=\"d1\">corruptible, to some extent <b>corrupted and struggling with sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>God our Father</b> is <b>incorruptible</b> → born again as his children</li><li class=\"d0\">→ life where we love with a <b>pure heart</b></li><li class=\"d1\">as opposed to <b>corrupted forms of love and lusts</b> we lived in before</li></ul>"},{"t":2456.81,"lv":2,"h":"1 Peter 1:24-25 - The word endures forever","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v. 24: \"all flesh is as grass, all the glory of man like the <b>flower of the grass</b>; grass withers, flower falls, but the <b>word of the Lord endures forever</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">From <b>Isaiah 40</b> - Peter draws on the analogy</li><li class=\"d1\">all flesh = our lives in this world: \"here today, <b>dried up and gone tomorrow</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">word of the Lord, capital W = <b>Christ</b> - endures forever</li><li class=\"d0\">\"This is the word which by the <b>gospel was preached to you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">gospel preached <b>Christ</b> to them - \"<b>didn't preach the Bible</b> to them\"</li></ul>"}]});
